  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ES-C317-algo",
      "sequence": 4970,
      "title": "algo — some, built out of other",
      "headword": "algo",
      "romanization": "algo",
      "gloss": "something — Latin aliquod, ali- plus quod, \"some other what\"; the same ali- is in alias, alien and altruism",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:b4ab617224c17a79",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What was res nata? (A thing born.) And what does todo do in front of a feminine word? (It becomes toda.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nada — the answer when there is none."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "todo — a great deal more than this."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "AL-go, two beats. The g is hard, as in goat. see reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "algo — \"something.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin aliquod: ali- plus quod. Quod is \"what.\" Ali- is \"other.\" Together: some other what — a thing not yet specified."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That ali- is worth collecting, because English has taken it repeatedly. An alias is an other name. An alien is from another place. To alienate somebody is to make them other. Altruism is caring about the other one."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So Spanish builds \"something\" out of \"other.\" That is not obvious at first, but it is sound. To say something is to say: not this particular thing — some other one, unnamed."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Algo has one shape and keeps it. It stands for a thing nobody has named yet, and a thing with no name has no gender to agree with."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The first of four indefinites."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Algo is a thing. The next one is a person, and it is built from exactly the same piece."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "algo",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *algo*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "sí, algo",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *sí, algo*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "algo, then nada",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *algo*, then *nada*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does ali- mean? (Other.) What is an alias? (An other name.) And why does algo have only one form? (The thing it stands for has no name yet, so no gender.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C317-alguien",
      "sequence": 4980,
      "title": "alguien — the same piece, now a person",
      "headword": "alguien",
      "romanization": "alguien",
      "gloss": "somebody — Latin aliquem, ali- plus quem, \"some other whom\"; algo's exact partner, built for people",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:098e7ce67675bd21",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does ali- mean? (Other.) And which part of res nata survived? (Nata, the born part.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "algo — the same word, for things."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la enfermera — one somebody you can already name."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "AL-guien, two beats. The gu is a hard g; the u is silent and only there to keep it hard. see reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "alguien — \"somebody.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Take algo apart once more: ali- \"other\" plus quod \"what.\" Now swap the second half. Quem is \"whom\" — the person form. Ali- plus quem is aliquem: some other whom."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Algo and alguien are the same construction built twice, once for things and once for people. Latin drew that line and Spanish kept it exactly. Algo is about a thing. Alguien is about a person. They are never swapped."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The spelling holds one trap. Gu before e or i is a hard g, and the u is doing no work of its own — it is a spacer, put there to stop the g going soft. You meet the same spacer in guitarra and guerra. So this word is \"AL-gyen,\" not \"AL-gwyen.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Like algo, it does not change. One shape for a man and the same shape for a woman, because the whole point of the word is that you do not yet know which."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The pair is complete."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two words for some: one for what, one for who, both built on other. And each of them has an opposite waiting."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "alguien",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *alguien*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "alguien, por favor",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *alguien, por favor*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "algo, then alguien",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *algo*, then *alguien*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is the quem in aliquem? (Whom — the person form.) What does the u do in alguien? (Keeps the g hard; it is silent.) And which of the pair is for things? (Algo.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C317-nadie",
      "sequence": 4990,
      "title": "nadie — nada's twin, and this one is about people",
      "headword": "nadie",
      "romanization": "nadie",
      "gloss": "nobody — Latin nati, \"born ones\", from homines nati, \"people born\"; the exact twin of nada, which was a thing born",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:8a56a455ff54ee2a",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which of algo and alguien is for people? (Alguien.) And what does ali- mean? (Other.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nada — its twin."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "alguien — what this answers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "NA-die, two beats. The ie at the end is one slide. see reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nadie — \"nobody.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Here is the twin that was promised."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nada came from res nata, \"a thing born.\" Nadie came from homines nati, \"people born.\" Same participle, same construction, same collapse. Latin said non homines nati, \"not people born,\" meaning not a soul. The non and the homines fell away. What was left was nati — the born ones — meaning nobody."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So Spanish has two words for nothingness and both of them are, underneath, the word born. One is singular and neuter, for things. One is plural and human, for people. The negation that made them negative has evaporated out of both."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Put all four together and the shape of the chapter appears. For things: algo and nada. For people: alguien and nadie. Some and none, twice over, one line each."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English has nati all over it and never once negatively: native, natal, nation (a people born in one place), nature, innate. Spanish is the language that took that root and made it mean absence."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four indefinites, in two matched pairs."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Algo and nada for things. Alguien and nadie for people. And both of the negatives are the Latin word for born."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "nadie",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *nadie*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "nada, then nadie",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *nada*, then *nadie*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "alguien, then nadie",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *alguien*, then *nadie*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What was homines nati? (People born.) What does nadie share with nada? (Both are the Latin word for born.) And which one is for people? (Nadie.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C317-otro",
      "sequence": 5000,
      "title": "otro — the other of exactly two",
      "headword": "otro",
      "romanization": "otro",
      "gloss": "another, other — Latin alter, which meant the other of a pair rather than any other at all",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:1cae2cc8f8f99bd4",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What was homines nati? (People born.) And what does the u do in alguien? (Keeps the g hard.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "uno... otro — the pattern this word gets built into."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "algo — built on Latin's other word for other."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "O-tro, two beats. The tr runs together and the r is a single tap. see reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "otro — \"other,\" \"another.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin had two words where English has one. Alius was \"some other, any other, one out of many.\" Alter was \"the other one\" — of a pair, specifically, when there are exactly two and you mean the one that is not this one."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish kept alter. Otro is alter with the l absorbed and the ending trimmed off."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And you have already met the alius side of the family. Algo and alguien are built on ali-, which is alius. So this one chapter contains both of Latin's words for other: one of them turned into \"something\" and \"somebody,\" the other turned into \"another.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English keeps alter in the open. To alter something is to make it other. Alternate things take turns at being the other one. An alter ego is your other self. And an altercation is a quarrel — two people, each being the other's problem."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Otro changes: otro for a masculine thing, otra for a feminine one, because it is describing the thing. Same rule as todo. An -o ending has to agree."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Both of Latin's others, inside one chapter."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ali- gave you algo and alguien. Alter gave you otro. One word left."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "otro",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *otro*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "otra vez",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *otra vez*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "otro, then otra",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *otro*, then *otra*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What was the difference between alius and alter? (Alius is any other; alter is the other of two.) Which one is otro? (Alter.) And what is an alter ego? (An other self.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C317-poco",
      "sequence": 5010,
      "title": "poco — a little, and the circle closes",
      "headword": "poco",
      "romanization": "poco",
      "gloss": "little, few — Latin paucus; English paucity is the noun and few is the same root through Germanic, and you already carry it inside tampoco",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:f53a5e50feed543d",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did alter mean? (The other of two.) And what does nadie share with nada? (The Latin word for born.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "tampoco — this word is already inside it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "mucho — its opposite."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "PO-co, two beats. The c is hard, as in cat. see reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "poco — \"little,\" \"few.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin paucus, \"few.\" The au flattened to o and the ending came off."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You have been carrying it for a long time without opening it. Tampoco — \"neither, not either\" — is tan plus poco: \"so little.\" Every time you have said tampoco you have been saying just as little, and poco is the second half of it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English has the Latin form in paucity, which means a shortage. It is a formal word and not common, but it is exactly this one."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And English few is the same root arriving down the Germanic road, after the p turned to f. That is the third time you have seen that shift in these four chapters. Pie and foot. Diente and tooth. Poco and few. Once you can hear a p becoming an f, a large part of English stops looking foreign."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Poco agrees, like todo and otro: poco and poca. It is measuring a thing, so it matches the thing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Twenty words, and the last one closes a circle."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You opened this run with el pie and a p turning into an f. You finish it with poco and the same shift. Between the two: the body, what it feels, and the small words that measure both."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "poco",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *poco*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "poco, then poca",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *poco*, then *poca*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "mucho, then poco",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *mucho*, then *poco*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is inside tampoco? (Tan and poco — so little.) Which English word is paucus through Latin? (Paucity.) And which is it through Germanic? (Few.)"
            }
          ]
        }
